ChordPro — charango chord voicings (The ChordPro Team)
===============================================================================

The `CHARANGO` table in `crates/chordpro/src/voicings.rs` (156 voicings,
returned by `charango_voicing`) is taken from the charango instrument file of
ChordPro, `lib/ChordPro/res/config/charango.json` (https://github.com/ChordPro/chordpro,
commit 1e1d7249, release R6.100.0). The file was contributed to ChordPro by
Edwin (GitHub user edwinjc). The tuning in the built-in `charango` configuration
preset (`crates/chordpro/src/config.rs`) is the one that file declares.

Copyright (c) 2010, 2018, 2023 The ChordPro Team (the notice in ChordPro's
README). ChordPro states that it is licensed under the Artistic License 2.0;
the voicings are redistributed under those terms. The license is copied in
`crates/chordpro/LICENSE-ARTISTIC-2.0.txt` and at
https://opensource.org/license/Artistic-2.0

How this copy differs from the upstream file (Artistic License 2.0, section 4):

  - The 156 entries keep the upstream chord names, base fret and fret
    numbers unchanged; the upstream table has 221 entries.
  - The 65 entries spelled with a flat root (Bb, Db, Eb, Gb, Ab), which repeat
    the sharp-rooted entries, are left out. `charango_voicing` resolves a flat
    spelling to the sharp-rooted entry instead.
  - The finger numbers (`fingers`) are not carried over.
  - A muted string, `x` upstream, is written `-1`.
  - The data is a Rust `static` table inside `chordsketch-chordpro`, not a
    JSON file read at run time.

The rest of ChordSketch is not a copy of ChordPro and stays under the MIT
License. ChordSketch is a different program with a different name, and
installing it does not prevent installing or running ChordPro (section 4(b)).
`crates/chordpro/src/chord.rs` reimplements in Rust ChordPro's rule for
spelling too-sharp keys as flats (`is_key_toosharp` in
`lib/ChordPro/Chords/Parser.pm`); that rule is covered by the same notice.
Elsewhere ChordSketch follows the behavior, directive names and configuration
keys of ChordPro R6.100 and R6.101 without containing its Perl source.

The guitar, ukulele and keyboard voicings in the same file were written for
ChordSketch and are not taken from ChordPro (the common open-position chords
agree with ChordPro's because they are the standard shapes).

ChordSketch's redistribution complies by retaining this NOTICE entry, shipping
the license text in `crates/chordpro/LICENSE-ARTISTIC-2.0.txt` (inside the
`chordsketch-chordpro` crate package) and in `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md` (inside
the binary distributions named above), and naming the source in the comments
of `crates/chordpro/src/voicings.rs`.


===============================================================================
ireal-reader and accompaniser — iReal Pro URL de-obfuscation and test data (MIT)
===============================================================================

`unscramble` and `obfusc50` in `crates/ireal/src/parser.rs`, which undo the
scrambling of an `irealb://` chart, are a Rust translation of `unscramble.js`
from pianosnake/ireal-reader (https://github.com/pianosnake/ireal-reader).
That routine follows `irealb_parser.lua` from ironss/accompaniser
(https://github.com/ironss/accompaniser). Several test URLs
(`crates/ireal/tests/fixtures/parser/`, the CLI tests, and the sample charts of
the playground and the iReal editor) are taken from the test corpus of
pianosnake/ireal-reader, some of them shortened or edited. The parser's
handling of the chart body follows the behavior of its `Parser.js`; the code is
ChordSketch's own.

Both projects are under the MIT License:

  - Copyright (c) Florin Alexandrescu (pianosnake/ireal-reader; the repository
    has no license file, its package.json names this author and MIT)
  - Copyright (C) 2013 Stephen Irons (ironss/accompaniser)

The MIT text, with the copyright lines above, is in
`crates/ireal/LICENSE-THIRD-PARTY.txt` (inside the `chordsketch-ireal` crate
package) and in `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`.


